# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3 have "∀x (removes(x) → mixed(x))" and "∀x (mixed(x) ∧ labour_his → joined_own(x))" and "∀x (joined_own(x) → property(x))"; locke-labour.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (removes(x) → mixed(x))"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is true, which the second allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is true. Is S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.2 have "∀x (joined_own(x) ∧ enough_left(x) → excludes(x))"; locke-labour.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (removes(x) ∧ mixed(x) ∧ labour_his ∧ enough_left(x) → excludes(x))"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is true. Is S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", true then?
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3 have nothing from it; locke-labour.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (labour_his ∧ mixed(x) → joined_own(x))" and "∀x (excludes(x) → property(x))"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" is true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: locke-labour.logic.r3 has "∀x (removes(x) → property(x))"; locke-labour.logic.r3.2 has "∀x (removes(x) ∧ enough_left(x) → property(x))"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows; locke-labour.logic.r3.3 has "∀x (removes(x) ∧ enough_left(x) → property(x) ∧ excludes(x))"
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.2 put it at S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property."; locke-labour.logic.r3.3 at S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others."
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", rather than S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others."?
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/readings: U1: locke-labour.logic.r3.2 takes for granted "for every x, if x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men, then x is his property"; locke-labour.logic.r3 and locke-labour.logic.r3.3 do not
locke-labour.logic.r3: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r3.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "for every x, if x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men, then x is his property"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
locke-labour.md:1: argue/negation: locke-labour.logic.r3 P6 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P6 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "for every x, if (he hath joined to x something that is his own and there is enough, and as good, left in common for others besides x), then x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/negation: locke-labour.logic.r3.2 P1 (S1): the sentence says "no", but P1 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "the earth and all inferior creatures are common to all men"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/negation: locke-labour.logic.r3.2 P5 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P5 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "for every x, if (he hath joined to x something that is his own and there is enough, and as good, left in common for others besides x), then x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/negation: locke-labour.logic.r3.3 P6 (S4): the sentence says "no", but P6 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "for every x, if (he removes x out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in and he hath mixed his labour with x and the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his and there is enough, and as good, left in common for others besides x), then x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men"
locke-labour.md:1: argue/negation: locke-labour.logic.r3.3 C (S4): the sentence says "no", but C denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "for every x, if (he removes x out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in and there is enough, and as good, left in common for others besides x), then (x is his property and x has something annexed to it that excludes the common right of other men)"
